<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amasya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amaseia, Amasia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amasya is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, in the narrow gorge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesilirmak">Yeşilırmak</a> river. As ancient Amaseia it was a capital of the kings of Pontus, whose rock tombs line the cliffs, and the birthplace of the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>. Later held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> it was a seat where young princes trained as governors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amasya.jpg" alt="Amasya" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmtzngn34 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ye%C5%9Fil%C4%B1rmak2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
